    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im working on some walnut cabinet joinery and im having a bit of an issue. i'm cutting dovetails but the wood fibers are splintering right at the tips of the pins every time i pare them back. it's making the joints look messy and i'm worried about the structural integrity. any advice on chisel sharpness or how i should be managing the grain to prevent this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This splintering usually indicates your chisel isn't sharp enough or you're pushing into the wood rather than pulling the sawtooth through it. To stop the pins from breaking, ensure your chisel has a razor-sharp edge and create a tiny cleaning-out glint on the back of the pin before you start paring out the waste. Finally, manage the grain by working with the fiber flow rather than cutting straight across it, applying only light, controlled pressure as you pull the waste away.",
