    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip on the cleaning-out glint. i think i've been pushing a bit too hard. im using a standard 1/4 inch bevel edge chisel right now. do you think i should switch to a different type of chisel for the fine paring, or is it more about the angle of the bevel? also, the walnut is pretty dense, does that change how i should approach the grain?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your 1/4\" bevel-edge chisel is perfectly fine for fine paring if you have already conditioned the edge for release. The key to working dense walnut is raising the lips so the chisel flattens out naturally against the grain, allowing you to lift waste rather than exert downward force. You don't necessarily need a different chisel type, but ensuring your angle of bevel sits correctly against the grain will significantly reduce that frustrating splintering. Just remember that patience and light pressure are just as important as the tool itself when tackling hardwood joints.",
