      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, look for distinct grain splits, stress cracks, or a rough texture running across the waste area where the sharp edge will go. When these signs appear, the wood tends to tear easily along those fracture lines if you aren't careful with the bevel setup. For walnut, it's often best to raise the lips slightly and/or reposition your stance so the chisel spine aligns with the grain splits, effectively making them cut away cleanly rather than splintering.",
